## 3️⃣ Test Way 3: Password Reset Token Leakage via Referer Headers
This checks if the application inadvertently leaks the generated secret reset token to a third-party domain when the user clicks embedded assets. [5, 6] 
## 📋 Step-by-Step Execution

   1. Trigger a legitimate password reset for your own account and open the received email.
   2. Click the password reset link, navigating to the final screen containing the input boxes for your new password (e.g., https://1win.com).
   3. Open Burp Proxy -> HTTP History and review all outgoing external connections triggered automatically by that page (e.g., analytics scripts, external images, or third-party fonts). [7] 

## 🔍 Response Verification Criteria

* Vulnerable Outcome ($750 - $1,000 Info Disclosure): Inspect the headers of the outgoing external connections. If any external domain shows a Referer header mapping the token query value:

Referer: https://1win.com

The token has leaked out of scope. Third-party logs can pick this up, allowing malicious external operators to re-use the token and take over user accounts. [8, 9] 

